{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And thus shall you eat it</b> etc. That is, in a manner ofordinariness and contempt, but not like other consecrated offerings, which are eaten in a manner of honor. And some say: the commandment of eating it is upon haste. <b>מצות — you shall eat it</b> refers back to expound also: they shall eat it on this night — מצות, in a manner of speed. <b>On bitter herbs they shall eat it</b> — they shall eat with it a bitter herb, so that everything is eaten at once: bondage, deliverance, freedom. The bitter herb is a remembrance of וימררו (and they embittered). The pesach is a remembrance of the deliverance, for at the time of the plague He פסח (passed over) them. The מצות are a remembrance of freedom, for the dough did not have time to leaven until they were redeemed. <b>You shall not eat of it נא</b> — for through this a person's soul loathes it and he leaves it, and it comes to the state of נותר. <b>Boiled in water</b> — for it does not cook as quickly in water as in fire. And furthermore, through cooking in water there are crumbs in the meat and it comes to the state of נותר, and all this is because of haste. Another interpretation: <b>you shall not eat of it</b> boiled in water, even though it was already cooked in fire, for thus <b> boiled</b> means roasted by fire, as in ויבשלו את הפסח. <b>Roasted by fire</b> — so that it cooks quickly, for in fire there is no barrier between it and the fire, and therefore they call it in the vernacular האט״א, meaning speed. It went back and wrote <b>roasted by fire</b> to exclude all liquids, and to exclude what is boiled in a pot without liquids, and all of them are forbidden; only roasted by fire alone. And it is not comparable to a widow and divorcee — profaned by harlotry — for only a virgin, as they say: he violates a positive commandment; for there it is written את אלה לא יקח, to teach that the negative applies only to those specified, but here יאכלוהו is not written. <b>Its head on its legs and on its innards</b> — roast it all at once, and if he makes of it two kinds of food, that is not a manner of haste, and perhaps it will come to the state of נותר. <b>And you shall leave none of it until morning</b> — it is not the way of people whose intention is to go on a journey to leave over for the needs of the next day. <b>And what is left of it — in fire you shall burn</b> — for you will go on your way and not eat it, and if any is left, dogs will eat it. Or it will be thrown away in contempt, and this is an ordinary manner, and it is consecrated meat. Here Rashi explained: what does Scripture mean by saying <b>until morning</b> a second time? To say that it is not burned on the festival but on the next day. And one should not object: let the positive commandment of 'in fire you shall burn it' come and push aside the negative commandment of labor on the festival — for the established ruling is that the festival is a positive commandment, as it is written 'on the first day, a solemn rest,' which implies cessation, and 'you shall do no labor,' and a positive commandment does not push aside a negative commandment and a positive commandment." "<b>And your staffs in your hands</b> — to drive the donkeys, as we find: ויך את האתון במקל." "<b>And you shall eat it in haste</b> — for in haste you shall go out of Egypt." "<b>It is a pesach to YHWH.</b> Rashi explained: the offering is called pesach on account of the skipping, etc. And you — perform all its rites. That is, the Holy One, blessed be He, did what was incumbent upon Him, and you likewise do what was incumbent upon you in the commandments of the pesach. And similarly Rashi explained regarding השקיפה ממעון קדשך מן השמים."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The abbreviation (אך) [או] appears to reflect a variant between witnesses; the bracketed או ('or') is adopted as the reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:22.219092093Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}